s intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The added to is not part of the expression.","B":"The particle cannot precede the verb in this structure.","C":"This separable use permits **verb + noun object + particle** while keeping let in the past.","D":"The object cannot be placed before the verb in this ordinary statement pattern."}}},{"id":"ed60c568-0be7-5935-af39-d867e550711b","topicSlug":"phrasal-verbs","level":"B2","prompt":"Keep the phrasal verb after did not: “They did not ____ down the team.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"to let"},{"id":"B","text":"lets"},{"id":"C","text":"been let"},{"id":"D","text":"let"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"Only the verb changes form; after **did not**, use its **base form**.","explanation":"After did not, the verb is base **let**; the particle **down** stays unchanged.","keywords":["phrasal-after-did"],"skill":"phrasal-after-did","contextKey":"phrasal-verbs-49","fingerprint":"a4988f54334b0b538c3c3e7346e4918ece6ba970bd0a045e25696ac7a77ffd2c","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-phrasal-order"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Only the verb changes form; after **did not**, use its **base form**.","After did not, the verb is base **let**; the particle **down** stays unchanged.","Choose **let**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Did not takes a bare infinitive.","B":"The next verb cannot carry a third-person -s after did.","C":"Been cannot follow did to build this negative.","D":"After did not, the verb is base **let**; the particle **down** stays unchanged."}}},{"id":"625ce8b8-62c5-5997-a2be-4e0b15411e3a","topicSlug":"phrasal-verbs","level":"B2","prompt":"In “Please back up the computer files”, what does the phrasal verb mean in this context?","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"make a safety copy of the computer files"},{"id":"B","text":"submit the homework"},{"id":"C","text":"distribute the leaflets"},{"id":"D","text":"transfer the keys to another person"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Use the **whole verb + particle**, and check its meaning against the object.","explanation":"Here **back up** means **make a safety copy of the computer files**. Learn this verb–particle combination as a unit.","keywords":["phrasal-meaning"],"skill":"phrasal-meaning","contextKey":"phrasal-verbs-50","fingerprint":"9987b2ca79c7cbd6fdb0a48807c974fb0ff1fd865a4e5a550ab1dd31f7f12ab2","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-phrasal-order"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Use the **whole verb + particle**, and check its meaning against the object.","Here **back up** means **make a safety copy of the computer files**. Learn this verb–particle combination as a unit.","Choose **make a safety copy of the computer files**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Here **back up** means **make a safety copy of the computer files**. Learn this verb–particle combination as a unit.","B":"That would describe “hand in the homework”, not the expression used here.","C":"That would describe “hand out the leaflets”, not the expression used here.","D":"That would describe “hand over the keys”, not the expression used here."}}},{"id":"527ca560-fefa-5b47-ac73-ed9e71a44450","topicSlug":"phrasal-verbs","level":"B2","prompt":"Replace “the computer files” with “them” in this separable phrasal verb: “Please back up the computer files.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"back up them"},{"id":"B","text":"them back up"},{"id":"C","text":"back them to up"},{"id":"D","text":"back them up"}],"answerId":"D","hint":"The object is now a **pronoun**, so place it **in the middle**.","explanation":"An object pronoun goes **between the verb and particle**: back them up.","keywords":["pronoun-placement"],"skill":"pronoun-placement","contextKey":"phrasal-verbs-50","fingerprint":"655d8536e229a1b91612d8845d73fc45d56d825a5233f54d4f47f558f167012c","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-phrasal-order"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The object is now a **pronoun**, so place it **in the middle**.","An object pronoun goes **between the verb and particle**: back them up.","Choose **back them up**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"With this separable verb, the object pronoun must come before the particle.","B":"This puts the object pronoun before the imperative verb.","C":"To is not part of this phrasal verb.","D":"An object pronoun goes **between the verb and particle**: back them up."}}},{"id":"0af4a4a8-84c0-57eb-ab13-cbb8379c3664","topicSlug":"phrasal-verbs","level":"B2","prompt":"Move the noun object between verb and particle, preserving the past tense: “They backed up the computer files.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"They backed the computer files to up."},{"id":"B","text":"They up backed the computer files."},{"id":"C","text":"They backed the computer files up."},{"id":"D","text":"They the computer files backed up."}],"answerId":"C","hint":"A **noun object** can separate this verb and particle.","explanation":"This separable use permits **verb + noun object + particle** while keeping backed in the past.","keywords":["noun-object-separation"],"skill":"noun-object-separation","contextKey":"phrasal-verbs-50","fingerprint":"d9181dc7a439ec94da0226778c129358cf4fdd317f9962a4729cb977fb627ae7","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-phrasal-order"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["A **noun object** can separate this verb and particle.","This separable use permits **verb + noun object + particle** while keeping backed in the past.","Choose **They backed the computer files up.**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The added to is not part of the expression.","B":"The particle cannot precede the verb in this structure.","C":"This separable use permits **verb + noun object + particle** while keeping backed in the past.","D":"The object cannot be placed before the verb in this ordinary statement pattern."}}},{"id":"ab7de8db-a3ef-58fb-a523-b33bd9ab1c01","topicSlug":"phrasal-verbs","level":"B2","prompt":"Keep the phrasal verb after did not: “They did not ____ up the computer files.”","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"been back"},{"id":"B","text":"back"},{"id":"C","text":"to back"},{"id":"D","text":"backs"}],"answerId":"B","hint":"Only the verb changes form; after **did not**, use its **base form**.","explanation":"After did not, the verb is base **back**; the particle **up** stays unchanged.","keywords":["phrasal-after-did"],"skill":"phrasal-after-did","contextKey":"phrasal-verbs-50","fingerprint":"3d77e7e2f9a3eec156efe4b7687d3b245ee69d20273df0ac2ee939f94c819348","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-phrasal-order"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Only the verb changes form; after **did not**, use its **base form**.","After did not, the verb is base **back**; the particle **up** stays unchanged.","Choose **back**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"Been cannot follow did to build this negative.","B":"After did not, the verb is base **back**; the particle **up** stays unchanged.","C":"Did not takes a bare infinitive.","D":"The next verb cannot carry a third-person -s after did."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Learn a verb and its particle together, including whether an object can split them.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"A verb plus particle can have a new meaning","explanation":"A phrasal verb combines a verb with an adverbial particle such as up, out or off. Its meaning may be literal or idiomatic. Learn the whole expression in context rather than trying to assign one fixed meaning to each particle.","pattern":"**Call off** a match = cancel it.","commonMistakes":["Off does not always mean remove: in call off, it contributes to the idiomatic meaning cancel."],"examples":["In “Please turn on the lamp”, what does the phrasal verb mean in this context? → **activate the lamp**","In “Please turn off the heater”, what does the phrasal verb mean in this context? → **deactivate the heater**"],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Separable transitive phrasal verbs","explanation":"With many transitive phrasal verbs, a noun object can come after the particle or between verb and particle. The 50 expressions in this bank are practised in their separable uses. A long noun object often sounds clearer after the particle.","pattern":"Turn **the light** off. / Turn off **the light**.","commonMistakes":["Not all multi-word verbs are separable; this pattern must be learned for the specific verb and meaning."],"examples":["Replace “the lamp” with “it” in this separable phrasal verb: “Please turn on the lamp.” → **turn it on**","Replace “the heater” with “it” in this separable phrasal verb: “Please turn off the heater.” → **turn it off**"],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Pronoun objects go in the middle","explanation":"With a separable phrasal verb, an unstressed object pronoun normally goes between verb and particle. Use turn it off, not turn off it. The verb carries tense; the particle does not change.","pattern":"She **turned it off**.","commonMistakes":["She turned off it is not the ordinary object-pronoun order for separable turn off."],"examples":["Move the noun object between verb and particle, preserving the past tense: “They turned on the lamp.” → **They turned the lamp on.**","Move the noun object between verb and particle, preserving the past tense: “They turned off the heater.” → **They turned the heater off.**"],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Inseparable and three-part verbs","explanation":"Prepositional verbs such as look after keep their preposition before the object: look after her. Three-part expressions such as put up with normally keep the whole sequence before the object. Some phrasal verbs are intransitive and have no object.","pattern":"Look **after her**. / Put **up with it**. / The plane **took off**.","commonMistakes":["Take off can be intransitive for a plane departing or separable for removing clothing; meaning changes its structure."],"examples":["Keep the phrasal verb after did not: “They did not **turn** on the lamp.”","Keep the phrasal verb after did not: “They did not **turn** off the heater.”"],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["**Call off** a match = cancel it.","Turn **the light** off. / Turn off **the light**.","She **turned it off**.","Look **after her**. / Put **up with it**. / The plane **took off**."]},"stage":"5 · Complex sentences","prerequisites":["verb-pattern-meaning"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
